  "step_3": "The decision reason requires calculating an aspiration level from all outcomes and comparing how often each lottery exceeds that level. This involves identifying all outcomes, computing their mean, and assessing probabilities of outperforming that mean. The individual’s report does not mention any such calculation, nor does it refer to specific outcome values (3000, 4000), probabilities, or comparisons relative to a reference point. While both the reason and the individual seem to focus on winning outcomes (i.e., non-zero results), the decision reason uses a structured, quantitative comparison based on an aspiration level, whereas the individual expresses a qualitative, motivational attitude about winning. There is no evidence that the individual computed or used the mean of outcomes, nor that they evaluated which lottery exceeds a calculated aspiration level more frequently. The similarity lies only in the attention to winning, but the cognitive process described does not align with the formal steps of the reason. Therefore, it is unlikely the individual used this specific decision reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 20
